  "text_md": "![](<4_pg451_images/imageFile1.png>)\n\n![](<4_pg451_images/imageFile2.png>)\n\nTHEORY OF EQUATIONS 435\n\nEx. 1. Prove that 5 is a root of the equation\n\nwv  3a? 182%+40=0.\n\nDividing by x 5, 1 3 18+440|5\n\n+ 5+ 10 40 14+2-  8; 0\n\nSince there is no remainder, 5 is a root of the given equation.\n\n- 507. If f(a) is divided by x  a, the remainder of the division\n\nis equal to f(a). (Remainder Theorem, Chapter XVI.)\n\nUsing the notation of § 505, we have\n\n(e a)Q(a) + R= f(a).\n\nSubstituting «=a, R=f(a).\n\nEx. 2. If f(x) = a*  20° 9a? + 2, find f(4).\n\nDividing f(a) by x 4,\n\neo Oia A 4p o-4 16\n\n14+2- 1- 4;  14\n\nHence S(4) = 14.\n\n- 508. The preceding method of substitution may also be dem\n\n\nonstrated as follows:\n\nv4  2a3 9274+0-27+4+ 2/4\n\n+4%734+8%7?-  42%  16\n\nat+2e2  a  44;  14\n\nJHE AVE peme\n\nx* = 47°, which added to the next term gives + 2 «°, 2x23= 84x?, which added to the next term gives x.\n\n« 2?=- 4z4, which added to the next term gives 4 x.  4xz=- 16, which added to the next term gives 14.\n\nTherefore J(4) =- 14.\n\n",
